Rules:
  [C1] Design tables for queries, not relationships.
  [C2] Use partition keys for data distribution.
  [C3] Use clustering keys for sorting.
  [C4] Avoid secondary indexes on high-cardinality columns.
  [C5] Use `TTL` for automatic expiration.
  [C6] Use lightweight transactions sparingly.
  [C7] Use appropriate replication factor.
  [C8] Monitor with `nodetool`.

Pros:
  - Linear scalability
  - High availability
  - No single point of failure

Cons:
  - eventual consistency
  - Limited query flexibility
  - Operational complexity

Alternatives:
  - ScyllaDB (faster)
  - DynamoDB (managed)
  - HBase (Hadoop)

State: +

